{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01GNiEnqyHHVS37Nf3aBuPeS","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→package com.test;\n     2→\n     3→import java.sql.Connection;\n     4→import java.sql.DriverManager;\n     5→import java.sql.SQLException;\n     6→\n     7→/**\n     8→ * Database service that handles database connections\n     9→ */\n    10→public class DatabaseService {\n    11→\n    12→    private String jdbcUrl;\n    13→    private String username;\n    14→    private String password;\n    15→\n    16→    public DatabaseService(String jdbcUrl, String username, String password) {\n    17→        this.jdbcUrl = jdbcUrl;\n    18→        this.username = username;\n    19→        this.password = password;\n    20→    }\n    21→\n    22→    /**\n    23→     * Create a connection to the database\n    24→     * @return Connection object\n    25→     * @throws SQLException if a database access error occurs\n    26→     */\n    27→    public Connection getConnection() throws SQLException {\n    28→        // Modern JDBC pattern - no need for Class.forName since Java 6\n    29→        // JDBC 4.0 drivers are automatically loaded from the classpath\n    30→        return DriverManager.getConnection(jdbcUrl, username, password);\n    31→    }\n    32→\n    33→    /**\n    34→     * Test the database connection\n    35→     * @return true if connection successful, false otherwise\n    36→     */\n    37→    public boolean testConnection() {\n    38→        try (Connection conn = getConnection()) {\n    39→            return conn != null && !conn.isClosed();\n    40→        } catch (SQLException e) {\n    41→            System.err.println(\"Failed to connect to the database: \" + e.getMessage());\n    42→            return false;\n    43→        }\n    44→    }\n    45→}\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is 